y worked or where congress  restrained at least the growth in  spending if not absolute cuts from 1990  to 2002 due to the efforts of President  George Herbert Walker Bush 41 and  William Jefferson Clinton we had tough  statutory pay-as-you-go rules  discretionary spending caps etc and by  and large they worked they helped  provide discipline they helped to give a  basis for people to say no why did they  here they disappeared because as you  know we had a surplus for four years  people thought that we didn't have a  problem anymore and frankly people  didn't want to be constrained with  regard to spending or tax cuts they  wanted to be able to do both and they  did a lot of both and now we're much  worse shape than we've ever been talk  about Social Security how big is the  problem and what do we do to to adjust  things Social Security does not face an  immediate crises but it is paying out  more and benefits than it's taking in  and cash revenues and and that will  never reverse right as well it's  supposed to it's supposed to reverse for  a few years less than five years after  we come out of the recession but it's  permanently going to be negative cash  flow starting in about 2017 you know we  need to recognize that it's prudent to  solve it sooner rather than later we  need to gradually increase the normal  and early retirement ages and then  indexing the increases in life  expectancy strengthen the benefit for  people into the poverty level provide  not quite as much for middle and upper  income and we need to add an automatic  savings account on top of a solvent  sustainable secure defined benefit  program let's talk about Medicare what  do we do with Medicare this this is the  thing that it will bring us to our knees  right well there's a lot of things with  Medicare we need to move away from  fee-for-service payment systems we're  going to go to more evidence-based  practices we can't write a blank cheque  for Medicare which we do now we need to  be more realistic about what type of  services should be covered under  Medicare we need to get out of the  business of providing subsidies for  billionaires and very wealthy people who  voluntarily sign up for Medicare as  outpatient physician payment and  prescription drug programs should we get  rid of the prescription drug benefit I  mean this is one of Bush's big legacies  it's a total black hole and it's shovel  it really shovels money from relatively  poor and young people to relatively  well-off and I think the federal  government was in the wrong business for  health care I think the federal  government over the next 20 years or so  should be in the business of making sure  that every American has preventive  wellness and catastrophic so guaranteed  insurability had group rates for those  things but beyond that I think that  there ought to be choices ought to be  options and Americans shouldn't be  required to take it beyond that  I think if we did something like that we  could do something would be affordable  and sustainable for our broad-based  societal benefit but what we have now  and frankly what we recently did I think  is going to compound our problem so that  and I mean obviously essentially what  the health care reform in a lot of ways  was that just passed is really creating  Medicare for everybody now or it's  certainly massively expanding the  government's role in regulating and  quite possibly ultimately in delivering  health care what do we do to pull that  back what how do you reach people to say  you know these systems are unsustainable  now Medicare is unsustainable adding  another you know trillion dollars in  healthcare spending over the next decade  or more is unsustainable what do you do  well what Washington just did is they  added a new wing to a health care  account house that it's structurally  unsound mortgage for more than it's  worth and headed for foreclosure sooner  or later people are going to realize  that and then we're going to have to  have an honest discussion debate with  the American people about what level of  health care coverage is appropriate  affordable and sustainable and how to  best finance it all the tough decisions  dealing with that were really punted and  I think it's probably gonna be 2013  before we come to make those decisions  but we must make them there is no way  that economic growth is going to save us  I know there's something called math in  order for us to be able to grow our way  out of this problem it would take  double-digit real economic growth every  year for decades it hasn't happened it's  not going to happen  and we also can't inflate our way out  either talked briefly about the Peter G  Peterson foundation what's its mission  and what's your role there well the  Peter G Peterson foundation is named  after a great American Pete Peterson who  puts his money where his mouth is and  his time where his heart lies we're  about promoting federal financial  responsibility we want to make sure it's  not an oxymoron we want to be able to  promote more you know responsibility and  accountability today so we can help  create more opportunity tomorrow we're  focused on fiscal issues savings issues  balance of payments issues basically  trying to help save the Republic are you  optimistic about the future or  pessimistic I'm an American so by  definition I'm optimistic at the same  point I'm I'm a realist and my view  is yes we can turn this thing around but  only if the first three words of the  Constitution come alive  we the people but aren't the people the  problem I mean they're the ones I mean  everybody wants something you know  you're talking about you know if it's a  75% subsidy on health care I'm paying a  quarter I'm getting a bucks worth of a  look human nature being what it is okay  everybody wants as much as they can get  and not have to pay for it but what they  have to understand is it's not just an  economic issue this is a moral issue we  are mortgaging t